thoughts when his friend, Jen, nudged him with a quizzed look. \"You look stumped,\" she commented.\n\nSam sighed, \"I am clueless. It's just that nothing special ever happens to me.\"\n\nJen shook her head. In all seriousness, Jen said softly but firmly, \"I don't think so. You are the good that happens to others.\" Sam stared blankly at her.\n\n\"All those little things you do. You have changed people's lives, even if you don't see it. You make people's day better. Maybe that's better than random good things happening to you. It means so much more when they show you gratitude,\" Jen continued.\n\nFor the first time, Sam looked back – not through his own eyes, but through the eyes of those he had helped. He remembered Mrs Raju smiling as she greeted her customers, her knees now aching less because of his help. He recalled Dan texting him a funny joke because he had a friend to talk to. It dawned on him that Jen was right and he was appreciative.\n\nSam finally realised that good things were indeed happening to him, just not in the way he had expected. Mrs Raju had once made his day by giving him some snacks after he helped her. The little girl would run to greet him joyfully each time they met at the playground. Dan's family also invited him to their family events.\n\nIt dawned on Sam that the acts of recognition could come in small ways. They were quiet in the warmth of a \"Thank you\", a grateful smile, or the unnoticed but undeniable impact on someone.",
